      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "title": "One table, one function, one allowlist",
        "synthesis": "Twilite already understands several address forms, but about twenty-two files parse them for themselves. The layer should be one pure function over a small table of schemes and aliases. It returns a description: the canonical address, how to fetch it, what kind of thing it is and whether it can be written. Aliases (`gh`, `yt`) exist for typing only. `web://` means \"a web resource\" and never replaces `http(s)://`. Markdown's link allowlist and every other caller are generated from, or routed through, that table.",
        "keyPoints": "• The pieces exist (`classifyTwiliteLink`, `resourceAuthority`, `graphRefLoader`, `convertTlzToFetchUrl`) but nothing joins them\n• `tlz://`, `local://` and root-relative paths belong in the table too\n• The Markdown allowlist would strip new schemes; derive it from the table\n• HTML-view links are not routed at all today\n• Canonical form on write, any form on read\n• Ship `github`, `web`, `http(s)` and the aliases first; `youtube` resolves and opens, representation later",
        "openQuestions": "• Should gh:// and yt:// be aliases of github:// and youtube://?\n• What does web:// mean, and what happens to http:// and https://?\n• How does web:// differ from tlz://, local:// and root-relative paths?\n• What should resolveAddress return?\n• Should a document store the address as typed, or the canonical form?\n• What safety rules must the resolver enforce?\n• What does youtube:// give Twilite that a link does not?\n• How does the resolver tell Save what it may write to?\n• What is the smallest first step, and does it come before the Page Template?",
        "notes": "A summary of recommendations. Nothing here is built.",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "0953fa34-568e-4500-88ed-dc18c9dede85"
        },
