      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "title": "Recommendation: keep all of them in the table and say what each is for",
        "answer": "The code already has three forms the discussion did not mention. `tlz://host/path` is a Twilite-served resource: `Browser.js` maps it to `https://host/path`, or to the current origin when the host is missing or localhost. `local://name.node` is a file opened from this machine, which the Toolbar saves back through a file handle. Root-relative paths such as `/library/…` are mapped by `lib/contentPaths.js` onto the bundled corpus or the `twilite-zone` repository.\n\nThe `tlz://` rewrite is nearly the one `web://` needs, so the open decision is how they differ. `tlz` says \"served by Twilite\", with the localhost-to-current-origin rule; `web` says \"any web resource\". If the difference is not worth two schemes, `tlz://` could become an alias, but that would take the localhost rule away from a spelling people already use.",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "summary": "tlz, local and root-relative paths already exist and must be in the table; tlz and web overlap.",
        "notes": "",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "0953fa34-568e-4500-88ed-dc18c9dede85"
        },
